+  "calc.222": "Your own time, or your accountant's",
+  "calc.223": "Finding, checking, permits and SGK paperwork are",
+  "calc.224": "done by us",
+  "calc.225": "This is a long-term hire, not a stopgap",
+  "calc.226": "The one-off costs only look big if you think of the worker as temporary. In practice these hires stay for years — the permit renews with you, and every renewal is cheaper than the first year.",
+  "calc.227": "The permit ladder",
+  "calc.228": "1 year",
+  "calc.229": "+2 years",
+  "calc.230": "+3 years",
+  "calc.231": "The first permit is for one year, then it extends by two, then by three. That takes the same worker to six years on your payroll. After that the permit keeps renewing on the same basis, and once they reach eight years of legal residence in Turkey they can apply for an open-ended permit.",
+  "calc.232": "Why they stay",
+  "calc.233": "The permit is tied to your workplace, so they cannot move to the employer down the road mid-contract. They relocated for this job, their family plan depends on it, and a clean renewal record is what lets them keep working here. Leaving early costs them far more than it costs you.",
+  "calc.234": "What that is worth",
+  "calc.235": "Every time someone walks out you pay again — advertising, interviews, the notice period, three months of training and a line running below capacity. A worker who stays three years skips all of that twice over, and by year two they know your machines better than a new local hire ever will in month one.",
+  "calc.236": "The one-off cost, spread over time — per worker",
+  "calc.237": "Permit fees + flight, paid once",
+  "calc.238": "Per month if they stay 1 year",
+  "calc.239": "Per month if they stay 3 years",
+  "calc.240": "Renewals cost only the state fee — no new flight, no new search, no retraining. That is why employers who have done this once keep the same people instead of starting again every season.",
+  "calc.241": "We say it openly: if you can fill the job locally, do that — it is simpler. This route is for the jobs that stay empty.",
+  "calc.242": "The short answer",
+  "calc.243": "Salary and SGK are",
+  "calc.244": "exactly the same",
+  "calc.245": "for a local and an overseas hire. Only two things differ.",
+  "calc.246": "1 · What overseas costs extra",
+  "calc.247": "state fees, once",
+  "calc.248": "Plus one flight. Both are paid a single time — the permit then renews with you, cheaper each year.",
+  "calc.249": "2 · What you get for it",
+  "calc.250": "The job actually fills.",
+  "calc.251": "At the same registered wage local applicants keep looking; these candidates apply and stay.",
+  "calc.252": "Location is not a problem.",
+  "calc.253": "Workers move to the factory, farm or site — small cities included.",
+  "calc.254": "Skills tested first.",
+  "calc.255": "Trade-tested candidates, ready now — no months of empty shifts.",
+  "calc.256": "They stay for the contract.",
+  "calc.257": "The permit is tied to your workplace, so you stop re-hiring and re-training.",
+  "calc.258": "Paperwork is ours.",
+  "calc.259": "Sourcing, checks, permit filing and SGK admin are done by us.",
+  "calc.260": "Hiring foreign students already in Turkey",
+  "calc.261": "This is a different route with its own rules: no flight, no consulate step, and the person is already living in your city. The limits depend entirely on what they are studying.",
+  "calc.262": "Associate & bachelor students",
+  "calc.263": "Ön lisans · Lisans",
+  "calc.264": "Only",
+  "calc.265": "after the first year is finished",
+  "calc.266": "— the prep year and the first year do not count.",
+  "calc.267": "Part-time only",
+  "calc.268": "— up to 24 hours a week.",
+  "calc.269": "Students on a Turkish state scholarship may not be allowed to work while they study.",
+  "calc.270": "Master's & doctorate students",
