+  "blogarticle.024": "In this article",
+  "blogarticle.025": "Turkey's factories, hotels, farms and construction sites are all competing for the same shrinking pool of local labor. Hiring from overseas is the practical answer — but the work permit process stops many employers before they start. This guide walks through it, end to end.",
+  "blogarticle.026": "Key takeaways",
+  "blogarticle.027": "The employer applies, not the worker — and salary below the ministry minimum is the #1 rejection reason.",
+  "blogarticle.028": "You need 5 Turkish employees per foreign hire, plus 100,000 TL capital — shortage sectors get exemptions.",
+  "blogarticle.029": "Realistic timeline: 4–8 weeks end to end, including ~30 days of ministry review.",
+  "blogarticle.030": "A licensed agency can file and track everything — you never touch a form.",
+  "blogarticle.031": "Who can hire foreign workers in Turkey?",
+  "blogarticle.032": "Work permits are applied for by the",
+  "blogarticle.033": "employer",
+  "blogarticle.034": ", not the worker. The Ministry of Labor evaluates your company before it evaluates the candidate. Two criteria matter most:",
+  "blogarticle.035": "5 : 1 rule",
+  "blogarticle.036": "Five Turkish employees registered at the workplace for each foreign worker you hire.",
+  "blogarticle.037": "Capital / turnover",
+  "blogarticle.038": "Paid-in capital of at least 100,000 TL, or gross sales that meet the ministry's threshold.",
+  "blogarticle.039": "Sectors with proven labor shortages — agriculture, manufacturing, tourism — benefit from exemptions and quotas that a licensed agency can apply on your behalf.",
+  "blogarticle.040": "Documents you'll need",
+  "blogarticle.041": "Signed employment contract",
+  "blogarticle.042": "Worker's passport copy",
+  "blogarticle.043": "Diploma or skill certificate",
+  "blogarticle.044": "Company tax & activity records",
+  "blogarticle.045": "Current SGK service statement",
+  "blogarticle.046": "Online application form",
+  "blogarticle.047": "The application, step by step",
+  "blogarticle.048": "Job offer & contract.",
+  "blogarticle.049": "The employer and candidate sign a contract that states role, salary and duration.",
+  "blogarticle.050": "Online application.",
+  "blogarticle.051": "Filed through the Ministry of Labor's e-permit system; the worker applies at the Turkish consulate in their country in parallel.",
+  "blogarticle.052": "Ministry review.",
+  "blogarticle.053": "Evaluation typically takes about 30 days; missing documents restart the clock.",
+  "blogarticle.054": "Approval & fees.",
+  "blogarticle.055": "Permit and card fees are paid; the permit doubles as a residence permit.",
+  "blogarticle.056": "Arrival & SGK.",
+  "blogarticle.057": "The worker enters Turkey with a work visa and must be registered with SGK within 30 days of starting.",
+  "blogarticle.058": "Mistakes that get applications rejected",
+  "blogarticle.059": "Salary below the ministry's minimum for the role — the most common rejection reason.",
+  "blogarticle.060": "Contract details that don't match the online application.",
+  "blogarticle.061": "Applying while the 5:1 employee ratio isn't met.",
+  "blogarticle.062": "Missing consulate appointment on the worker's side.",
+  "blogarticle.063": "As a licensed İŞKUR agency, JobsAdmire files and tracks the entire permit application for you — most of our clients never touch a single form.",
